<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hieronymus Wolf</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1516–1580</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hieronymus Wolf was a German humanist scholar and librarian in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> who edited and translated Greek authors. His Corpus Historiae Byzantinae of 1557, a collection of medieval Greek historians, did much to popularise the term 'Byzantine' for the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, whose people had called themselves Romans. He is often regarded as a founder of Byzantine studies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hieronymus-wolf.jpg" alt="Hieronymus Wolf" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Colossus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hieronymus_wolf2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
